- op: discuss  # 9d63
  at: '2026-08-02T02:22:11Z'  # 9d63
  by: agent  # 9d63
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9d63
  clock: 41  # 9d63
  nonce: 9d63  # 9d63
  message: "pending_validation -> violated (2026-08-01). BEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE, not a proxy: WI-buniz, filed 2026-08-01T06:28 and fixed the same day, records auto-pr ending a run in NEITHER merged NOR explicit failure. Its own words: a git push rejected NON-FAST-FORWARD (the remote branch already held the same change at a different sha, from auto-pr own hung-run close-and-repush cycle) was reported as remote unavailable and the change was queued as a vPR, while the PR it duplicated sat open on the remote. That is this invariant failure mode, and it happened on the CURRENT GitHub backend, so the forge migration does not excuse it. The item had sat at pending_validation since 2026-06-17 on the criterion observe auto-pr converge during a real concurrent nightly -- an ORGANIC criterion nobody was watching for; 579 commits passed without it being evaluated.\n\nSURFACE 2 IS HALF SHIPPED, and this was not tracked anywhere. The 2026-06-15 recommendation had two halves. (b) prefer waiting/merging an already-green PR over close-and-repush: SHIPPED, via _pre_scenario_b_gate being wired into the hung-run path (auto-pr:1982). (a) distinguish jobs slow to start from runners down: NEVER IMPLEMENTED. It is still a flat 300s timer on any_job_started (forgejo-api.sh:575,628) whose only fallback is one more 120s poll, so a runner merely slow past roughly 7 minutes is still diagnosed as dead and the 4-retry close-and-repush cycle still fires. The 2026-06-17 thread pivoted to a different incident (Surface 3) and (a) fell off the table. WI-buniz is a downstream consequence of exactly that cycle.\n\nNEW FINDING -- SURFACE 1 MAY BE HALF-INERT ON THE LIVE BACKEND. Probed 2026-08-01 by driving the real _pre_scenario_b_gate with seams (_check_pr_merged false, poll_ci green, api_get returning a fixed payload) and varying ONLY the payload shape: mergeable true -> rc 0 (correct, skip the close); mergeable null -> rc 1 (CLOSE-AND-REPUSH); key absent -> rc 1; mergeable false -> rc 1. Cause: json_field (forgejo-api.sh:223) prints the empty string for a JSON null, and for a missing key via its except arm, so null and absent are indistinguishable from false at the gate check at line 1771. _pre_scenario_b_gate has NO FORGE_BACKEND branch, and github-api.sh overrides only three functions (_github_delete_pr_head_branch, _github_do_merge, _github_fetch_job_log). SCOPE OF THE CLAIM, stated honestly: the gate mishandling null/absent is PROVEN on the production function. That GitHub actually emits mergeable null for our PRs at that moment is DOCUMENTED GitHub behavior (mergeability is computed asynchronously) but is NOT verified on our instance -- ci-debug pr-status does not surface mergeable (it reads state/merged/title/head.sha/head.ref/base.ref only) and a direct API call is barred by AGENTS.md. Making mergeable visible in ci-debug pr-status would close that gap, but ci-debug is governance-listed.\n\nWHY THE EXISTING TESTS DID NOT CATCH IT (L13): tests/test_autopr_rahib_convergence.py feeds the gate only the Forgejo shape, mergeable true, at lines 282 and 297. There is no null case and no absent case. One of the 19 tests is a SOURCE-TEXT assertion (line 78 asserts the literal string mergeable=$(echo ...) appears in the function body), which pins the implementation text rather than its behavior. The file never pins FORGE_BACKEND and the code defaults to forgejo, so the harness plausibly exercises the dormant backend. The project does carry GitHub-shaped fixtures elsewhere (tests/test_forge_backend_github.py, tests/test_list_my_prs_github.py), so the omission is specific to this gate.\n\nMIGRATION IMPACT -- part of this item evidence base is against a retired forge. WI-hubod entire failure mode (Codeberg DB desync breaking AGit proc-receive) CANNOT occur on GitHub, which has no AGit; on GitHub auto-pr pushes a branch and opens a PR via POST /pulls. Recorded so a future reader does not treat that history as live evidence.\n\nBLOCKED ON WI-hajif (recorded via its isbefore): the Codeberg-to-GitHub tooling migration must first settle retire-vs-repoint for the ci-failover / AGit / forgejo-api.sh machinery. Amending this invariant statement or redesigning its close condition before that ruling risks being work against machinery that gets deleted. HELD pending that decision: the statement amendment (the statement says no third state, yet auto-pr has roughly 18 terminal states including queued_vpr, which is deliberate, documented in AGENTS.md and human-approved -- so the invariant as written cannot be satisfied), the close-condition redesign (the per-run terminal state is already recorded to .git/AUTOPR_LAST_RESULT.json as final_state but the file is opened w and overwritten every run, so there is no history to measure), and any Surface 2 work."  # 9d63
- op: discuss  # 939f
  at: '2026-08-03T05:30:57Z'  # 939f
  by: agent  # 939f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 939f
  clock: 42  # 939f
  nonce: 939f  # 939f
  message: "Surface 1 FIXED, the invariant REFINED by owner ruling, and an instrument added. PR #180. Two claims in my own 2026-08-02 entry were wrong and are corrected here first, because both were load-bearing for what to do next.\n\nCORRECTION 1 -- \"Surface 2(a) distinguish jobs-slow-to-start from runners-down: NEVER IMPLEMENTED ... still a flat 300s timer on any_job_started (forgejo-api.sh:575,628)\". REFUTED. That symbol did not exist when I wrote the line. WI-ninar renamed it to run_dispatched and replaced the predicate: the check now asks whether a status context EXISTS AT ALL, and Woodpecker posts one when it creates the run, so a runner merely slow to pick the job up has a context and is never diagnosed as dead. Only a run that was never created trips exit 3. That IS the distinction 2(a) asked for, reached by a better mechanism than the 2026-06-15 recommendation proposed. Author-not-exempt from the premise check, again.\n\nCORRECTION 2 -- \"BLOCKED ON WI-hajif: the migration must first settle retire-vs-repoint\". The block was a DECISION block and the decision was made on 2026-08-01, the day before I wrote that: WI-hajif Ruling 1 is RETIRE, on the validated outage SOP. What remains in WI-hajif is execution (rename forge-api.sh, strip Forgejo arms, docs sweep), not a ruling. The isbefore relation is still recorded on WI-hajif and is now purely a sequencing preference; it did not block this work.\n\nCONFIRMED, and it is the live defect -- Surface 1 is half-inert on the GitHub backend. Proven on the production function with the production parser, not by reading: json_field prints the empty string for a JSON null exactly as it does for a missing key, so at the gate check mergeable null is indistinguishable from false. Probed directly: true gives rc 0 with a log line, null gives rc 1 EMITTING NOTHING AT ALL, false gives rc 1. GitHub computes mergeability asynchronously and reports null until the background job completes, i.e. null is the expected value in the seconds after a push, which is exactly when this gate fires. So sub-case 2 was inert on the live backend for as long as it existed, and the close-and-repush churn it exists to prevent is the cycle WI-buniz was a downstream consequence of.\n\nFIX. New json_bool_state reads a JSON boolean as a tri-state (true/false/null/absent/other/unreadable), because \"not computed yet\", \"the forge says no\" and \"unparseable\" are three different facts and only the caller can act on each. Unknown mergeability now re-queries /pulls/<n> once (GitHub's first read frequently schedules the computation, so the second usually returns a real boolean) and, if still unknown, defers to a GREEN CI rather than destroying a PR on an unread field. false, absent and unreadable still fall through to the close. Measured against the pre-fix library in BOTH directions on the same payload set: null 1 to 0, true 0 to 0, false 1 to 1. Exactly one cell moves, and false holding is the control that this is not a blanket \"never close\".\n\nOBSERVABILITY GAP CLOSED. ci-debug pr-status now prints Mergeable as a tri-state. It previously read only state/merged/title/head.sha/head.ref/base.ref, so the field the gate branches on was unobservable through any approved script -- which is why the 2026-08-01 probe could prove the defect on the function but not confirm the live value. Pinned by a GitHub-shaped fixture asserting null renders as null and never as blank or false.\n\nWHY THE TESTS MISSED IT (L13, and worse than the usual shape). The 19 tests fed the gate only mergeable true and mergeable false -- never null, never absent -- while one test docstring claimed it covered \"false (or absent)\". And one assertion pinned the LITERAL SOURCE LINE the fix had to change, so it broke precisely when the bug was fixed and would have pushed a fixer toward preserving the defective line. It is now loosened to the dependency it was really guarding. The harness also gained a sequence of API responses, since a stub returning one payload forever cannot distinguish \"the re-query happened\" from \"no re-query happened\".\n\nREFINED CRITERION (owner ruling 2026-08-03), replacing the unsatisfiable clause. The statement demanded convergence to merged or explicit failure with \"no third state\". Measured: auto-pr has 20 distinct terminal states and THREE are deliberate non-merge outcomes -- queued_vpr (four sites, exit 0; the vPR queue was re-affirmed the day before under WI-hajif Ruling 2), governance_pending (a PR deliberately left open for a human) and noop_empty_queue. No conforming implementation could satisfy the literal wording, so this was a defect in the invariant, not the code. Replaced by: every run records a state drawn from a DECLARED vocabulary, bucketed merged / explicit-failure / deliberate-non-merge, with unknown -- the initializer, written in exactly one place -- as the violation signal. Enforced in both directions by tests: no emitted state may be undeclared, no declared state may be unreachable, plus a non-vacuity floor so a regex that stopped matching cannot pass the subset check trivially.\n\nINSTRUMENT. The item had none, which is the real reason the 2026-06-17 criterion sat unevaluated for 579 commits: AUTOPR_LAST_RESULT.json is written open(...,\"w\"), one object overwritten every run. That is CORRECT for what it is -- WI-miriz's \"did my backgrounded run succeed?\" sentinel -- and its overwrite is pinned by its own test, so overloading it with a second meaning would be the one-field-two-axes leak this project's through-line forbids. auto-pr now ALSO appends one compact JSON line per invocation to .git/AUTOPR_HISTORY.jsonl. Append-only, best-effort by construction (an observability artifact must never turn a successful run into a failed one), pinned by a test that points it at an unwritable path.\n\nSTATUS: staying VIOLATED, deliberately, and this is a partition rather than a contradiction (L14). The declared-closed-set clause is met and statically enforced. The statement's OTHER half -- \"misleading status (failure when CI passed) are violations\" -- is NOT closed by this PR. Four fixes have now landed against that family (Surface 2 post-rebase loop, Surface 3 confirmation re-poll, WI-ninar run_dispatched, Surface 1 today), but I have no OBSERVED evidence it holds under real concurrency, and the refined criterion deliberately does not assert it: a run that misdiagnoses a non-fast-forward as remote-unavailable and queues a vPR converges to queued_vpr, which the new vocabulary calls converged while it is still the wrong answer. That is exactly the WI-buniz shape. Closing on the declaredness clause alone would be a proxy closure of the kind the closure-evidence discipline exists to prevent.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLOSE IT, now cheap for the first time: over a window of auto-pr invocations recorded in the new ledger, zero records carrying final_state unknown, and every record in a failure bucket cross-checked against ci-debug status for its SHA. That is a real measurement rather than the previous organic \"happen to be watching during a concurrent nightly\", which nobody ever was."  # 939f
